export const DE_C1_S05_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-nachruf-auf-vorrat": [
    `In the offices of the "Wiener Abendblatt" there was a cupboard nobody talked about. It stood on the third floor, right next to the coffee machine, and it held the obituaries of people who were still alive. Anyone important enough to be mourned on the arts page one day had a folder there: actors, bishops, former ministers, a ski racer. Thomas Wallner, who had been in charge of these folders for eleven years, privately called the cupboard "the holding pattern".`,
    `Nobody had foreseen that one day one of those waiting would phone. "Good afternoon, this is Hedwig Lanner," said a voice he knew from a hundred evenings at the Burgtheater, smoky and a touch too clear, as if she were still on stage. "I hear you've already written me. I'd like to read it." Wallner choked on his coffee. How did she know, he asked. "Young man, I am ninety-one. At my age you find out everything, only usually too late."`,
    `He should have refused; it broke every rule, and the editor-in-chief would not, to put it mildly, have praised him. Even so, the next afternoon he was sitting in a flat in the Josefstadt, among photographs, theatre posters and a canary that eyed him suspiciously. Frau Lanner, her glasses on the tip of her nose, read the text he was secretly proud of: "the great tragedienne", "a voice like dark velvet", "her life belonged to the stage".`,
    `"No," she said, and laid the sheet on the table as if it were a badly fried schnitzel. "It's all true, and it's all wrong." She had only been the great tragedienne, she said, because nobody had ever given her a comedy. And her life had by no means belonged to the stage. "The stage rented me, for three hours in the evening. The rest belonged to me."`,
    `So began what Wallner would later call a dictation. Every Tuesday he took the tram to the Josefstadt, and every Tuesday she cut something. Out went the prizes, the rings of honour, the Federal President who had kissed her hand in 1987. In their place came things no reader of the "Abendblatt" would have expected: that she had played cards passionately badly; that she had learned to swim at forty, in the Old Danube, while children laughed at her; that she had eaten apricot dumplings only out of a sense of duty and had actually loathed them.`,
    `There was only one point she never talked about. In her CV there was a gap between 1961 and 1963, and when Wallner asked about it, she changed the subject or watched the bird eat. Wallner, journalist enough to be annoyed by this and polite enough not to show it, didn't ask again. In any case he had stopped feeling like a journalist on these visits. More like a tailor fitting an old lady with a dress she would never put on.`,
    `In February the text was finished. It was shorter than before, cheekier and, as Wallner had to admit to himself, a great deal better. Frau Lanner read it twice, nodded, and then pushed an envelope across the table to him. "This goes in as well. But only once I can no longer object." He was not to open it before then. She would notice if he did; actresses noticed things like that.`,
    `She died in May, on a morning when it was properly warm in Vienna for the first time. Wallner opened the envelope at his desk, next to the cupboard that now held one folder fewer. Inside was a single sentence, written with a fountain pen: "Between 1961 and 1963 she was happy. Beyond that it is nobody's business." He put it at the end without changing a word. The editor-in-chief struck it out again because it explained nothing. Wallner wrote it back in. In the end it stayed, and it was, as people told him later, the sentence that was talked about most in the coffee houses.`,
  ],
  "de-die-letzte-telefonkabine": [
    `There is news that shakes a place, and there is news that merely offends it a little. The letter the municipality of Hinterrüti in the Toggenburg received last March belonged to the latter: the phone company would be removing the public phone box by the village square "as part of the modernisation of the network". They thanked us for our many years of trust. The mayor read the letter out at the assembly, and for a moment it went quiet, the way it goes quiet when someone announces that a distant uncle has died whom you last saw at a christening.`,
    `I too, I admit, made fun of the fuss at first. In this newspaper I wrote that the phone box was the only building in the village that nobody had ever entered, not even the priest. I wrote about faded stickers, about a phone book from 2004 in which half the people listed had long since moved to the cemetery, and about spiders that had settled in there with a calm any spa director would envy. For this I received two approving letters to the editor and a cake from the Looser bakery, which in Hinterrüti counts as a literary prize.`,
    `Then the technician came. He was a friendly man from Wil who had evidently done this job in twenty villages already and showed no more feeling about it than a dentist pulling a wisdom tooth. While he loosened the screws, I asked him, half joking, when anyone had last made a call here. He tapped around on his tablet and frowned. "Last Sunday," he said. "At seven in the evening. And the Sunday before as well. Every Sunday, actually, for years. Always the same number, always just under a minute."`,
    `A village of nine hundred people can put up with a lot, but not with a secret. Within two days three theories were going round: a secret love affair, a betting office abroad and, championed by the landlord of the "Rössli", a foreign agent, though it remained unclear what such a person would be spying on in the Toggenburg, apart from cheese prices, perhaps. I confess that I took part in this speculation, and with a zeal worthy of a local journalist but rather unworthy of a grown-up.`,
    `So the following Sunday, at ten to seven, I was standing behind the lime tree on the village square, where only a rectangular patch of lighter asphalt was left to recall the phone box. At seven on the dot Ruedi Brändle arrived, our retired postman, eighty-four years old, in his Sunday jacket, with a two-franc coin in his hand. He stopped in front of the empty patch and looked down at it for a long time, the way you look down at a grave on which someone has forgotten the flowers. Then he put the coin back in his pocket.`,
    `I should have withdrawn. Instead I stepped out from behind the tree, which didn't surprise him in the least; postmen always know who is standing where. "I call home," he said before I could ask. "Elsbeth is still on the answering machine. 'Hello, you've reached the Brändles, we're just out in the garden.' You can't call yourself from home, can you. And I never wanted one of those mobiles." He shrugged as if it were a technical detail. "On Sundays at seven we always used to watch the news together."`,
    `Since then I have stopped making fun of phone boxes. They were built, it turns out, not for the people who make lots of calls, but for the few who need exactly one. The phone company replied politely to my enquiry that the decision was irrevocable. I believe them. So every Sunday at seven Ruedi Brändle and I sit on the bench under the lime tree, and I lend him my mobile. Meanwhile I look at the pale patch in the asphalt. It takes just under a minute, and I have never listened.`,
  ],
  "de-taxi-ohne-ziel": [
    `Shortly after two the old lady got in at Dammtor, and Emre Kaya knew at once that the night was going to be a long one. She wore a hat of a kind nobody had worn for fifty years, and held her handbag on her lap like a child you don't want to wake. "Just drive," she said. Where to, then? "I'll tell you on the way."`,
    `Wonderful. Twenty-two years he had been driving a taxi in Hamburg now; he had taken drunks from the Reeperbahn to Pinneberg, witnessed a birth on the Köhlbrand Bridge and once driven a football star who had cried on the back seat. But passengers without a destination were the worst. Either they had no money, or they were up to something you'd rather not know about. Or they were confused, and then sooner or later you had to call the police, and the night was a write-off anyway. He switched on the meter and drove towards the Alster, because he couldn't think of anything better.`,
    `"To the fish market," she said at the Lombard Bridge. And at the fish market, where at this hour there was nobody about but a few gulls and a street sweeper, she said: "Now out along the Elbchaussee. Slowly, please." She looked out of the window as if she were reading something off it. At a villa in Othmarschen she had him stop. "This is where he picked up a shipowner, every Thursday, for seven years. The man never gave a tip. Not once." Who, he? In the rear-view mirror Emre saw her smile. "I'll tell you on the way."`,
    `They went on to Övelgönne, where she asked him to wait with the engine running while she stood on the Elbe beach in the dark and watched the container ships. Then to a snack bar in Altona that had closed long ago; it had once had the best currywurst north of the Main, she said. Then to the petrol station on the Ring 2, where, as she put it, you could have the most honest conversations in the city at four in the morning. Emre knew the petrol station. He knew the snack bar too. He had eaten there himself, in his first years, before it closed. Slowly a suspicion crept up on him.`,
    `"My husband drove nights for forty years," she said at last, as they waited at a red light on Willy-Brandt-Straße that was completely pointless at this hour. "Taxi. From seven in the evening to five. I saw him come in the morning and leave in the evening, and in between he had a whole second life that I only knew from the stories. I never rode along. He didn't want that. 'The night is no place for you, Inge,' he used to say." She paused. "The funeral is tomorrow at eleven. I thought I ought to see, just once beforehand, where he was all that time."`,
    `Emre said nothing. What could he have said? He thought of his own wife, who was asleep in Wilhelmsburg now and also knew his nights only from the stories, and only the funny ones at that. He thought of how often he had told her not to worry. The light turned green. He didn't drive off. Nobody honked behind them, because nobody was behind them.`,
    `Towards half past four, as it grew grey above the cranes in the harbour, he dropped her off in front of a red-brick house in Barmbek. The meter showed a hundred and twenty-eight euros and forty cents. She opened her handbag, but Emre had already pressed the button, and the numbers had vanished. "Colleague's rate," he said. She looked at him for a long time, as if she wanted to object, and then didn't after all. "He would have liked you," she said instead. "You drive just as slowly as he did."`,
    `On the way back Emre passed the petrol station on the Ring 2 once more. He didn't stop. He drove home, earlier than usual, and woke his wife to tell her what this night had really been like.`,
  ],
  "de-die-feelgood-managerin": [
    `When the management of Mainhafen Leasing GmbH hired a feel-good manager in January, the first thing Holger Brinkmann did was work out what she cost. He didn't mean any harm by it; he worked everything out, that was his job. For twenty-six years he had sat in Controlling, fourth floor, a view of a car park and, in good weather, of a bit of the Main. One annual salary, plus fruit, yoga mats and a so-called budget for team experiences, divided by a hundred and forty employees. He arrived at a figure that he told nobody but that gave him a certain grim satisfaction.`,
    `The feel-good manager was called Jasmin Yıldız, was perhaps thirty and, you had to give her that much, was not afraid of him. On the first Monday she put smoothies in the kitchenette; on the second she put up a "wall of appreciation" in the corridor, where you were supposed to stick notes praising colleagues. Holger stuck nothing. Appreciation, in his view, showed itself in correctly completed travel expense claims, not in green sticky notes.`,
    `Even so, she dropped by every day. Not for long, two or three minutes. She asked how he was, and he said "fine", which was a lie, and she said "good", which, he suspected, was a lie as well. Once she brought him a coffee, black, without asking how he took it. He would have liked to know how she knew. He didn't ask. You didn't start that sort of thing.`,
    `In March his wife moved out. Strictly speaking she had moved out years ago; only her things had taken until March. Holger told nobody, least of all his colleagues, who believed he slept in the office anyway. But when Jasmin put her head round the door one Thursday and asked how he was, he didn't say "fine". He said nothing at all. Without being asked, she sat down on the visitor's chair, on which nobody had sat since 2009, and stayed for an hour.`,
    `After that she stayed more often. They talked about figures, because that was all he could talk about, and at some point about everything else. He learned that she was actually a social worker, that she had taken the job because it paid twice as much as anything in her field, and that she couldn't stand the smoothies herself. She learned that as a young man he had wanted to study history. The "wall of appreciation" was almost empty by now, and Holger realised that he might be the only person in the whole building on whom her work was working.`,
    `In October the email from management arrived. Spending cuts. All "non-value-adding positions" were to be reviewed; would Herr Brinkmann please submit a cost-benefit analysis by the end of the month. Attached: a list of three posts. The first was the feel-good manager's. Holger read the email twice and then a third time, as if the text might change if you only looked at it long enough. Him, of all people. Of course him. Who else?`,
    `He took three weeks, twice the usual time. The analysis he finally handed in was a masterpiece: forty-one pages, flawless tables, footnotes with sources, a graph of staff turnover where you had to look closely to notice that the axis didn't start at zero. Conclusion: the post was indispensable; sick leave in Controlling had fallen by fifty per cent since January. That Controlling consisted of exactly two people, and that the other one had had a leg in plaster the year before, was in footnote seventeen, which nobody read.`,
    `The post stayed. Jasmin never found out why. In twenty-six years Holger Brinkmann had never massaged a single figure, and he felt he was entitled to one. The following Monday, when nobody was looking, he stuck a green note on the wall. On it was the figure he had worked out in January, neatly crossed out. Underneath, in his small, precise handwriting: "estimated too low".`,
  ],
  "de-das-fraeulein-vom-amt": [
    `In the autumn of 1928, at the Moabit telephone exchange, a hundred and twenty young women sat in long rows in front of the switchboards, each with a headset that began to pinch after three hours and a mouthpiece on her chest. There were exactly two rules, and Frau Krüger, the chief supervisor, repeated them every morning as if they were written in the Bible: you don't listen in. And you don't have private conversations. "Exchange here," you said, "go ahead?", and then you plugged in the line and kept quiet.`,
    `Grete Lindner, twenty-two, from a village in the Uckermark, kept to both rules until that evening in October when, at twenty to ten, a man's voice said: "Please connect me with the moon." She should have hung up. Instead she heard herself reply: "I'm afraid the moon is engaged, sir. Try again later." At the other end someone laughed, a short, surprised laugh, and the line went dead.`,
    `The next evening he wanted the North Pole. The evening after that, the Emperor of China. Grete reported that the North Pole was frozen and the Emperor had unfortunately been abolished. The conversations never lasted longer than half a minute, and Frau Krüger, who moved silently up and down the rows on her rubber soles, noticed nothing, or at least pretended not to. What was the harm? Half a minute. A bit of nonsense among a thousand connections to Charlottenburg and Schöneberg. It was, Grete felt, the only thing about this job that belonged to her.`,
    `Meanwhile a word was going round the exchange that nobody liked to say out loud: automatic. In other districts, people said, subscribers would soon dial for themselves, with a dial on the telephone, no young lady, no "exchange here", no headsets. Some of the girls would be moved to the long-distance service. The rest, well. Grete knew what "the rest" meant. Her mother in the Uckermark knew too, and wrote every week asking whether she wouldn't rather get married after all.`,
    `One Friday she broke the first rule. The man with the moon had earlier asked, quite ordinarily, for a number in Siemensstadt, and Grete, her heart pounding, left the switch open a moment too long. She heard enough. He talked about selectors and relays, about a trial run, about "Moabit in the spring". He was an engineer. He was building the machine that would replace her. Of course. Him, of all people. And she had offered him the moon.`,
    `When he asked that evening whether she could connect him with the Sahara, she said coolly, "For you, sir, there are no more connections," and pulled the plug. He called twice more. She let the indicator drop and did nothing. Frau Krüger stopped behind her and said quietly, so that nobody else could hear: "The machines are coming anyway, Fräulein Lindner. I've been through it once before, in Hildesheim. The only question is who loses their head over it." Then she moved on, on her silent soles, as if nothing had happened.`,
    `In April, on the last evening before the switchover, the exchange was half empty. At twenty to ten Grete's indicator dropped. "Exchange here, go ahead?" A pause, then the voice: "Please connect me with Fräulein Grete Lindner." She didn't ask how he knew her name; in Berlin you could find out anything if you knew someone at the post office. "Speaking," she said. From tomorrow, he said, anyone in Moabit could reach anyone without her. With one exception. "Sunday, four o'clock, Café Kranzler. That's a number no machine can dial. For that I need you."`,
    `Whether she went, Grete never told her granddaughters quite so directly later on. She only said, whenever talk turned to the old days, that she had made the best connection of her life without a cable. And whenever the telephone rang, right to the end she answered it with the same words: "Exchange here, go ahead?" Her husband, the engineer, found it funny every single time, and it was, so she claimed, the only joke he laughed at for fifty years.`,
  ],
};

